                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "While this game has great core gameplay, there are some aspects about it that don't quite land with me, even if these aren't too detracting from the overall experience. First of all, I feel like this is a rhythm game only on the most technical level. Usually rhythm games have a wide variety of different songs with levels designed around these songs. Not the case with this game; rather, it's moreso that every level is a variation on the main theme song of this game and that the rhythm aspect comes moreso that the obstacles you bypass and the items you collect all make different sounds that add to the song, but these different variants are hardly any different from each other within the same world. Admittedly the instrumentation will change a bit from world to world, but that's about it. This game also has a very absurdist type of humor with a highly verbose presentation that, most of the time, just doesn't land with me. There is a lot of padding in this game too. As you go through the game, each world will have keys that you can get which unlock routes to chests. The way you get these keys is by beating a Key Vault level, which are always placed around 3/4ths of the way through a world. So if you want all the costumes, you'll have to backtrack to previously beat levels to go through them again with newfound key access. Also, each world has two levels with alternate exits (one leads to the Key Vault level, the other leads to a level that unlocks a new character), so you inevitably have to beat those levels twice (once normal exit, once alt exit) if you want to get those side stages with the unlockables. Also with any level that has a Retro Cartridge, you get sent to the bonus stage and, after completion, you get sent back to the map and have to start the stage over again. Speaking of, there are these collectable Retro Cartridges, 5 in each world, that give you access to these Retro style challenge stages. For some reason it has 3 lives despite the fact that if you lose all 3 lives, you can just hop immediately back into it from the level select and there are no checkpoints in these stages anyways. Also a stage, while it will tell you if it has keys or costumes, will not tell you if it has a cartridge. They're easy to spot initially, but by the time you get to World 3, you may start missing them, so having a guide that tells you which stages they are all in is recommended in order to save you the headache of just trying to figure out which stages they're in. You do get a cool character if you beat all 25 (though sadly it's really hard to see it in the final world you get it in since it practically blends in with the background). The different levels within a world also hardly differentiate from each other aside from the rare unique setpiece (World 3 actually does the most with having unique setpieces and background elements and is honestly just the best world in general outside of World 5 having the best instrumentation). However, despite all of these complaints I've levied, I actually still quite enjoy this game as a whole. It's arguably, gameplay wise, the best auto runner I've played. Tight, responsive controls with very fun, engaging level design that keeps you on your toes. It can be pretty challenging at times, though I never had too much trouble with it. One thing to keep in mind is that this game reminds me a bit of Pizza Tower in that it's built around the people who are going to no life this game. It has all these mechanics that are involved in racking up as high of a score as possible and encourages those who truly ingrain themselves into this game to achieve a mastery of the games' levels and mechanics. Every gold you get adds score, every enemy dodged, skipping checkpoints gives a big score boost, you can dance in less busy sections to rack score, the bonus game at the end of a level if all gold is obtained adds to your score, a ton is there that is purely there for those who want to aim for as high of a score as possible. However, if you have no desire to go for high scores (like with me), then you don't necessarily have to sweat over getting all the gold, acing the bonus games, you can get all the checkpoints, etc., which actually can make things easier and less stressful for the casual player. If you like the core gameplay of Geometry Dash but perhaps the difficulty eventually got too brutal and you want a similar game with more balanced difficulty, I would highly recommend playing this game. Even if it has some quirks that hold it back a bit, the core gameplay is still very fun and I generally had a fun time the whole way through.",
